00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:26,320
So, let me run you through a couple of things that make designing the internet hard.

112
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,720
So, this is, again, things that make the internet a unique design challenge

113
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,080
that you might not have to wrangle with if you're designing something else,

114
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:37,920
like you're building, I don't know, a new computer, or you're building a chip.

115
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:40,400
These are things that you probably didn't have to deal with,

116
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:44,720
but with the internet, these are challenges that we care about, and they're special.

117
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:45,520
So, here's one.

118
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:47,440
The internet is federated.

119
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:51,440
So, I don't know about you, but there's no such thing as, like, internet incorporated.

120
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:54,800
There's no company that runs the entire internet.

121
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:00,000
In fact, the internet is actually made up of hundreds of thousands of different organizations,

122
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,560
companies, governments, that all have to work together to form the internet.

123
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:06,000
There's no single company.

124
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,880
So, AT&T is a company that works in the internet space.

125
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:11,920
China Telecom is a company that works in the internet space.

126
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:15,920
Verizon, UC Berkeley itself has people working on the internet.
Verizon，UC

127
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:21,040
So, all these people from all these different companies have to work together to build the internet,

128
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:25,440
and that's a challenge that a lot of other design fields don't have to deal with,

129
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:26,960
or computer science fields, I guess.

130
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:32,080
So, everyone has to cooperate in order for the internet to work on a global scale.

131
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:34,640
And so, this already introduces something interesting.

132
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,520
There are actually business incentives to the internet.

133
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:42,880
So, these companies, they want to make money, and that means that they're competing with each other.

134
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:48,480
And that means that, for example, even though AT&T and Verizon have to work together to give

135
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:54,080
internet connectivity to all of the US, AT&T and Verizon probably don't want to share

136
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,920
private information with each other.

137
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:59,280
They don't want to share their business secrets, because that would be bad.

138
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:03,520
So, already we see that this is something that you probably haven't had to think about

139
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:06,480
when you're, I don't know, programming something for your class.

140
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:08,720
You don't have to think about, what about my competitors?

141
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:09,920
What are they going to do?

142
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:12,960
But in the internet, we do care about business incentives,

143
00:08:12,960 --> 00:08:16,400
and that'll be a big part of something that we'll see later called BGP.

144
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:21,200
So, another challenge is the internet being federated, that is,

145
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:25,840
being made up of all these different operators, it also makes innovation hard.

146
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:31,600
So, if you think about any other field, like, I don't know, computers or phones or whatever,

147
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:34,000
if someone invents a new feature, that's great.

148
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,200
Like Apple just invented this brand new feature on their iPhone,

149
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:38,720
and so they're going to keep it secret.

150
00:08:38,720 --> 00:08:42,080
They're not going to share it with anyone else, and now they can sell their phones

151
00:08:42,080 --> 00:08:45,040
and beat everyone else because they have a brand new feature.

152
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:50,000
But in the internet, it's not so easy, because remember, the way that the internet works

153
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,560
is all of these operators have to work together to form the internet.

154
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:59,120
So, if you have a brand new feature, but none of your competitors implement the feature,

155
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:00,080
it's useless.

156
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:03,600
The whole point is that everyone has to run on the same infrastructure

157
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:05,440
and use the same protocols.

158
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:10,240
So, here's a case where already, it seems like the incentive to make something new,

159
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:11,200
it's kind of weird.

160
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:13,920
It's not like I can make something new and then sell it to the world

161
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:15,600
and hide it from my rivals.

162
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:20,160
If I make something new, I actually have to make sure other people support it too,

163
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:23,200
because we all are working together to form the internet.

164
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:24,720
It's not a single company.

165
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:28,720
So, already there's all these different incentives, and it makes it harder to innovate.

166
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:32,880
These are all things that we're going to see as obstacles or things to design around.

167
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:33,840
So, it's kind of cool.

168
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:37,840
Okay, what else is interesting about the internet?

169
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:41,920
It's scale-level, so hopefully I don't have to convince you the internet is very big

170
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:43,920
and it is constantly growing bigger.

171
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:46,400
The number of users just keeps increasing.

172
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:51,200
The number of things we put on the internet, the things that we send over the internet,

173
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:53,200
is also constantly increasing.

174
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:57,360
And the internet has to be able to handle tremendous scale,

175
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:01,200
scale that basically no other piece of software has to handle.

176
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:03,200
The internet is also evolving.

177
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:07,840
So, again, because people keep asking for more applications, different things,

178
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:09,920
the internet has to continually evolve.

179
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:14,240
So, for example, they invented email, and now the internet has to support email,

180
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:15,920
and then video streaming became a thing.

181
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:19,120
Now we have to support video streaming, and then AI became a thing,

182
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:21,920
and now we have to support AI training and so forth.

183
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:26,320
So, the internet itself is also constantly evolving based on what users want.

184
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:30,720
Something else about the internet that makes it hard is that it's diverse.

185
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:34,720
So, some users, or all users are different basically.

186
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:36,800
So, some users use a lot of data.

187
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:39,200
Some users don't use any data at all.

188
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:40,320
Some users are nice.

189
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:41,920
Some users are malicious.

190
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:45,920
So, everyone is a little bit different in what they want and what they need.

191
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:48,640
Same thing on, for example, the company side.

192
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:50,240
Some companies are serving video.

193
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:52,320
Some companies are serving emails.

194
00:10:52,320 --> 00:10:56,160
So, everyone wants a slightly different thing out of the internet,

195
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:01,920
and we have to somehow be able to support all these different devices, users, services,

196
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:04,480
each of which has their own special requests.

197
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:09,440
So, it's a really daunting challenge to try and service everyone's requests,

198
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:10,960
but it's something we're going to try to design.

199
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:14,640
Okay, here's one that, again,

200
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:17,200
maybe you haven't seen before when you're programming other things,

201
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,120
but the internet is asynchronous.

202
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:22,800
And what I mean by that is that the internet is all over the world.

203
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,280
So, it's not the case that when you update something,

204
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:27,760
someone else gets the update right away.

205
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:30,720
There's a speed limit in physics called the speed of light.

206
00:11:30,720 --> 00:11:33,760
Things cannot travel faster than the speed of light.

207
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:38,640
So, what that means is, for example, if I update something here in San Francisco,

208
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:41,360
well, then by the time it reaches New York,

209
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:46,800
even if it travels at the fastest possible speed with no obstacles along the way,

210
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:49,440
it would still take 13 milliseconds.

211
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:53,040
13 milliseconds is actually a really long time in computer world,

212
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:56,480
and if you have a CPU that runs at this particular frequency,

213
00:11:56,480 --> 00:12:01,600
that means that your CPU has already executed 42 million instructions

214
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:05,280
by the time your message has reached New York.

215
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:08,240
And you can imagine, if they send a message back to you,

216
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:10,560
that's another 42 million cycles.

217
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:12,960
So, by the time you get the information,

218
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,200
you have already done lots of other work,

219
00:12:15,200 --> 00:12:19,680
and the information that you receive is, by definition, outdated.

220
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:22,880
There's just no way to get instant updates from New York,

221
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:24,480
because it's too far away.

222
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:27,040
So, because the internet is built in all these different places,

223
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:29,680
things that you send over the internet,

224
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:32,400
they're going to be outdated, and we have to deal with that.

225
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:37,520
There's no way to instantly update someone on the other side of the country or the world.

226
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:41,840
Okay, a couple more challenges, and then I'll take questions.

227
00:12:41,840 --> 00:12:45,520
So, one challenge is, the internet has to handle failures.

228
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:48,480
It would be really bad if a server somewhere went down,

229
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:50,640
and the entire world just stopped working.

230
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:52,000
There's no more internet.

231
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,520
So, we have to build the internet in such a way that if something fails,

232
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:58,640
like a server goes down, or a router goes down, or something,

233
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:00,880
or there's a power outage in Berkeley,

234
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:04,400
the rest of the world should still be able to service the internet,

235
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:05,920
or access the internet.

236
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,040
So, we have to handle failures, and we have to handle it at scale.

237
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:12,160
So, for example, even if a whole city has a blackout,

238
00:13:12,160 --> 00:13:16,400
the internet should still continue working for everyone else in the world.

239
00:13:16,400 --> 00:13:19,520
So, wires could break, network devices could break,

240
00:13:19,520 --> 00:13:22,000
a city could go down in terms of blackout,

241
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
and the internet should still work.

242
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:27,040
And even weirder compounding this issue

243
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:30,560
is that when something fails, you might not know right away.

244
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:34,080
Remember the issue from before, where everyone is located far away?

245
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,800
By the time you find out that something has failed,

246
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:38,720
that news might be old.

247
00:13:38,720 --> 00:13:40,400
Who knows? They might be back up.

248
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:42,320
That news might be outdated.

249
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:46,880
So, combining this and the fact that information that you get is outdated,

250
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:48,640
it makes things really tricky.

251
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:51,680
And in fact, some say that the internet was the first system

252
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:55,840
that really had to handle failures at this kind of amazing worldwide scale.

253
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:58,640
So, when you're writing a program and it crashes, it's no big deal.

254
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:01,840
But when the internet, when people are writing the internet,

255
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:04,080
we got to make sure this thing does not crash,

256
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:09,600
has to work for basically all of time, which is pretty big challenge.

257
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:10,960
So, I mean, here's just an example.

258
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:14,080
If I have 50 links, and we'll talk about links next time,

259
00:14:14,080 --> 00:14:16,480
and if they all work 99% of the time,

260
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:19,600
there's already a 39% chance that something fails.

261
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:22,560
So, if I expand this to the entire world,

262
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:24,320
things are failing all the time.

263
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:26,800
There's like millions of wires in the world.

264
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:29,520
Right now, as we're speaking, something is failing,

265
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:31,280
but the internet is still working.

266
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:32,480
How do they build that?

