336
00:17:29,680 --> 00:17:32,240
So, what are we going to design in this class?

337
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:35,120
So, we're going to be spending basically the whole class

338
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,520
designing tons of protocols.

339
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:38,640
What are protocols?

340
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:40,720
Protocols are basically a set of rules

341
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:42,400
for how two people communicate.

342
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:44,080
And since the internet is all about

343
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:46,320
having people across the world communicate,

344
00:17:46,320 --> 00:17:49,040
well, then stands to the reason that most of the internet

345
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:51,840
is basically built of lots of protocols.

346
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:54,400
So, we're going to build lots of protocols for communication,

347
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:56,480
and with them, we'll get the internet.

348
00:17:57,040 --> 00:17:58,160
So, what is a protocol?

349
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:00,640
It's a set of rules on how two people communicate,

350
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:03,680
and you can split it into two things.

351
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:07,600
So, a protocol consists of syntax and semantics.

352
00:18:07,600 --> 00:18:09,520
So, what are these things?

353
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:12,320
Well, syntax tells me what does the message mean?

354
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:15,680
So, when someone hands me a big pile of zeros and ones,

355
00:18:15,680 --> 00:18:16,720
what are they talking about?

356
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:17,760
What does the zero mean?

357
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:18,880
What does the one mean?

358
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:22,800
How do I decode this to get useful, meaningful messages?

359
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:24,640
That's what the syntax is.

360
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:27,600
And then the semantics say, when I get a message,

361
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:28,480
what do I do?

362
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:30,720
So, if someone tells me resend,

363
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:32,000
okay, great, what does that mean?

364
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,440
Does it mean I should resend now?

365
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:35,040
Does it mean I resend later?

366
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:35,840
What does that mean?

367
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:39,280
So, we have to figure out what the messages mean,

368
00:18:39,280 --> 00:18:43,440
and also, what do I do in response to those messages?

369
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:45,200
Put those two things together,

370
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:46,640
you get a protocol.

371
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:47,840
So, to give you an example,

372
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:50,800
what's your protocol for asking a question?

373
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:52,320
Well, you should raise your hand,

374
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:54,160
and we both know what that means.

375
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:55,840
I know it means you have a question.

376
00:18:55,840 --> 00:18:57,520
You know it means you have a question.

377
00:18:57,520 --> 00:18:59,280
So, we both agree on the syntax.

378
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:04,320
That putting your hand up is syntax for I have a question.

379
00:19:04,320 --> 00:19:05,680
And then what do you do when you raise your hand?

380
00:19:05,680 --> 00:19:07,040
You don't just start blabbing.

381
00:19:07,040 --> 00:19:08,960
You wait for someone to acknowledge you and say,

382
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:10,320
oh, you have a question.

383
00:19:10,320 --> 00:19:13,120
And then you can start talking and ask your question.

384
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:14,480
And if, for some reason, we don't see you,

385
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:16,080
maybe you'll wave your hand more.

386
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:18,960
You say, excuse me, or something like that.

387
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:21,920
But these are all rules that I agree on, you agree on,

388
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:23,280
and that's how we communicate.

389
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:25,440
It's by using this protocol.

390
00:19:25,440 --> 00:19:27,760
And it turns out designing protocols

391
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:30,720
is what we're going to be doing basically the whole semester.

392
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:33,200
And it's actually not as easy as it seems.

393
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:37,040
There's actually whole organizations whose entire job

394
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:39,200
is just to debate protocols all day.

395
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:42,480
So, if that sounds fun, you can go join the IETF,

396
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:44,800
Internet Engineering Task Force.

397
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:46,720
And basically their entire job is just

398
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:48,880
to make protocols, standardize them

399
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:51,920
so that everyone knows how it works, and then publish them.

400
00:19:51,920 --> 00:19:54,720
So, if you ever see RFCs, those are documents

401
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:57,040
that just contain the protocol.

402
00:19:57,040 --> 00:19:59,200
And everyone talks about it and argues about it.

403
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:00,640
And if everyone thinks it's good,

404
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:03,840
then it becomes a new internet protocol.

405
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,280
So, this is what we're going to be doing

406
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:07,440
for basically all of this semester.

