495
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:19,920
for example, UC Berkeley,

496
00:23:19,920 --> 00:23:22,400
or a town, or my house,

497
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:26,160
and I can connect up everyone in that local area.

498
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:28,000
So, for example, I have these four circles

499
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,400
representing four computers

500
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:32,560
in some close by space,

501
00:23:32,560 --> 00:23:35,040
and I can just take a wire, for example,

502
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:37,520
and I can just hook them all up to the same wire.

503
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:39,760
Now, they are all connected together,

504
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:41,920
and they can send messages to each other.

505
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:43,840
So, we call this a link,

506
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:46,400
because it's connecting up multiple machines,

507
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:47,520
and if I use links,

508
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:50,400
I can connect up everyone in a local area.

509
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:52,240
For example, I could take some wires,

510
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:54,880
I could connect all of us in here to the same wire,

511
00:23:54,880 --> 00:23:58,960
and then we would all be connected on a particular link.

512
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:00,160
And what's great about a link is,

513
00:24:00,160 --> 00:24:01,920
now we can exchange data,

514
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:04,160
and people have a fancy term for data,

515
00:24:04,160 --> 00:24:05,280
they call it packets,

516
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:07,840
but all that is, is it's a bunch of ones and zeros

517
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:09,600
representing something you wanna send.

518
00:24:09,600 --> 00:24:10,480
It could be a picture,

519
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:11,680
it could be a video,

520
00:24:11,680 --> 00:24:12,800
it could be an email,

521
00:24:12,800 --> 00:24:13,920
or whatever it is,

522
00:24:13,920 --> 00:24:15,520
you encode it in a packet,

523
00:24:15,520 --> 00:24:17,840
and you send it on the link.

524
00:24:17,840 --> 00:24:20,160
So, we built something interesting.

525
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:23,040
We used our first technology, single wire.

526
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:24,720
We connected a bunch of people,

527
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:28,960
now we have a way to exchange data between people.

528
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:31,360
Okay, but there is a problem,

529
00:24:31,360 --> 00:24:34,720
which is, I stopped at this step right here.

530
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:36,080
How is this going to scale up?

531
00:24:36,080 --> 00:24:37,680
How can I possibly take a wire

532
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:40,400
and connect every single person in the world?

533
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:42,800
Or how do I take an optical fiber

534
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:45,600
and connect that to every single person in the world?

535
00:24:45,600 --> 00:24:46,480
I can't.

536
00:24:46,480 --> 00:24:48,880
So, instead, I have to think about,

537
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:52,240
how do I connect people across further distances?

538
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:54,480
Let's say, for example, I have two towns,

539
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:56,480
here's a town, it's got four people in it,

540
00:24:56,480 --> 00:24:58,800
they're all happily connected with the link,

541
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:01,040
and then here's another town with four people

542
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:03,360
also happily connected with the link.

543
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:05,360
How do I connect these two towns together?

544
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:06,640
Say, someone from here

545
00:25:06,640 --> 00:25:09,120
wants to send a message to someone from here,

546
00:25:09,120 --> 00:25:11,200
how do they communicate?

547
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:12,480
So, I mean, something you could try

548
00:25:12,480 --> 00:25:14,240
is you could try to link up everyone

549
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:15,440
with a bunch of links.

550
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:17,760
So, go install a bunch of wires between people,

551
00:25:17,760 --> 00:25:20,000
between every pair of houses,

552
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:21,760
or every pair of nodes,

553
00:25:21,760 --> 00:25:23,760
but hopefully you can already see

554
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:26,080
this is going to get very complicated,

555
00:25:26,080 --> 00:25:28,480
very expensive, very quickly,

556
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:30,560
so we don't want to do this.

557
00:25:30,560 --> 00:25:33,200
So, instead, I'm going to go borrow

558
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:34,880
from my postal analogy

559
00:25:34,880 --> 00:25:37,920
and bring in something else called a post office.

560
00:25:37,920 --> 00:25:40,160
Have people even been to post offices?

561
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:41,760
Because you're also young, I don't know.

562
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:43,840
But a post office is a building,

563
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:46,720
you can go there and drop off your mail

564
00:25:46,720 --> 00:25:48,320
and they will deliver the mail

565
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:50,000
to its destination for you.

566
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:51,440
So, here's what I'm going to do.

567
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:53,040
I'm going to introduce a post office

568
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:54,160
in the first town

569
00:25:54,160 --> 00:25:56,800
and introduce a post office in the second town.

570
00:25:56,800 --> 00:25:58,400
And instead of doing the thing from earlier

571
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:02,960
where I draw wires between every single pair of computers,

572
00:26:02,960 --> 00:26:04,960
I'm just going to connect the two post offices.

573
00:26:04,960 --> 00:26:08,240
So, for example, drop a wire between them

574
00:26:08,240 --> 00:26:09,840
or in the postal analogy,

575
00:26:09,840 --> 00:26:12,800
tell the mailman to go between these two buildings.

576
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:14,960
And so, what's great about this is now,

577
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:18,000
for example, if this person wants to talk to this person,

578
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
instead of directly sending a message

579
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,320
because we already saw that can't scale,

580
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:24,640
you can send a message to your post office

581
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:26,480
and then what's your post office going to do?

582
00:26:26,480 --> 00:26:28,640
It's going to forward the message.

583
00:26:28,640 --> 00:26:32,240
So, pass it along this link to the other post office

584
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:34,240
and then that post office looks at the letter and says,

585
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:35,520
oh, it's for this person.

586
00:26:35,520 --> 00:26:37,520
Let me drop it off for that person

587
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:39,920
at their final destination.

588
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:42,000
So, by using this post office approach,

589
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,560
I only needed a single extra link

590
00:26:44,560 --> 00:26:47,520
and now both of these towns are connected.

591
00:26:47,520 --> 00:26:48,080
Which is great.

592
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:50,880
Thoughts on how I built this?

593
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:54,800
Okay, that's great.

594
00:26:54,800 --> 00:26:56,160
I connected two towns.

595
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:57,440
If I've connected two towns,

596
00:26:57,440 --> 00:27:00,560
what is stopping me from connecting three or four

597
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:01,840
or all of them?

598
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:03,920
And in fact, that's what we do.

599
00:27:03,920 --> 00:27:05,760
So, we're going to go crazy

600
00:27:05,760 --> 00:27:07,680
and we're going to connect all the towns

601
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:10,320
with all sorts of post offices and houses.

602
00:27:10,320 --> 00:27:12,720
So, you can imagine this is like a map of the world.

603
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:15,040
I have all these different houses lying around

604
00:27:15,040 --> 00:27:16,400
and all these different towns.

605
00:27:16,400 --> 00:27:19,040
There's all these different post offices connecting them.

606
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:21,040
And if I connect everyone up together

607
00:27:21,120 --> 00:27:22,960
in the whole wide world,

608
00:27:22,960 --> 00:27:24,800
I get the internet.

609
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:26,080
And I built it in a way

610
00:27:26,080 --> 00:27:28,000
where I took small building blocks

611
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,720
and worked my way up something like this.

612
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:32,400
And if you squint at this a little bit,

613
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:34,080
you'll see why we call the internet

614
00:27:34,080 --> 00:27:36,240
a network of networks.

615
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:38,000
That's a term that people like to use.

616
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:39,200
So, what does that mean?

617
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:40,560
Well, if you stare at this,

618
00:27:40,560 --> 00:27:42,320
we have all these little towns

619
00:27:42,320 --> 00:27:45,120
and each little town is kind of doing its own thing.

620
00:27:45,120 --> 00:27:46,720
So, here's a network.

621
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:47,600
Here's a network.

622
00:27:47,600 --> 00:27:48,400
Here's a network.

623
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:49,440
Here's a network.

624
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:52,000
Each little town is doing its own little thing.

625
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,480
And then all the towns connect to each other

626
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:55,760
to form the internet.

627
00:27:55,760 --> 00:27:58,000
So, why do we call this a network of networks?

628
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,640
It's because all of these little individual pieces

629
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:01,760
form a network.

630
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:04,560
And then we connect all the subnetworks up

631
00:28:04,560 --> 00:28:07,440
into a big, gigantic network.

632
00:28:07,440 --> 00:28:08,880
That's the internet.

633
00:28:08,880 --> 00:28:10,480
That's your first peek of the internet.

634
00:28:11,040 --> 00:28:12,880
It's this little crappy picture on the slide.

635
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:14,000
That's the internet.

636
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:14,320
Kind of.

637
00:28:15,440 --> 00:28:16,080
Okay.

638
00:28:16,080 --> 00:28:16,880
Pause for questions.

639
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:32,000
Okay.

640
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:32,240
Okay.

641
00:28:33,760 --> 00:28:34,480
Great.

642
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:37,120
So, to break down this diagram a little bit more

643
00:28:37,120 --> 00:28:38,960
and to introduce some more terminology,

644
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:40,160
it's the first lecture.

645
00:28:40,160 --> 00:28:41,520
There's a lot of terminology.

646
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:42,240
Sorry.

647
00:28:42,240 --> 00:28:44,640
But if you look at this picture,

648
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:48,080
we're not going to stop saying post offices and houses

649
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:51,440
because the internet is not actually post offices and houses,

650
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:55,120
but in fact, the internet is end hosts and routers.

651
00:28:55,120 --> 00:28:57,760
Those are the fancy words that we're going to use.

652
00:28:57,760 --> 00:29:00,960
So, in this class, when I draw an end host, I draw a circle.

653
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:04,080
I tried my best to make this always true,

654
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:05,120
maybe some exceptions,

655
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:08,720
but 99% of the time when you see a circle in this class,

656
00:29:08,720 --> 00:29:10,480
the circle is the end host.

657
00:29:10,480 --> 00:29:12,480
These are the machines that want to communicate

658
00:29:12,480 --> 00:29:13,680
over the internet.

659
00:29:13,680 --> 00:29:15,920
So, as an analogy, these are the houses.

660
00:29:15,920 --> 00:29:17,280
People live in the houses

661
00:29:17,360 --> 00:29:19,200
and they want to talk to each other.

662
00:29:19,200 --> 00:29:22,480
In the internet world, the end hosts are, for example,

663
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:24,240
your laptop is an end host,

664
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:26,000
your phone is an end host,

665
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,800
Google server that answers your search queries,

666
00:29:28,800 --> 00:29:30,000
that's an end host.

667
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,480
These are the people that want to talk to each other

668
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:33,680
and have things to say.

669
00:29:34,400 --> 00:29:36,400
By contrast, these switches,

670
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:38,160
they're like the post offices.

671
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:41,840
So, the post office, it's not really there to talk to you.

672
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:42,560
I don't know about you,

673
00:29:42,560 --> 00:29:44,880
but like I'm not talking to my post office,

674
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:48,160
but the post office exists to forward packets

675
00:29:48,160 --> 00:29:49,680
toward their destination.

676
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:52,480
So, the post office, its entire job for existing

677
00:29:52,480 --> 00:29:54,400
is to receive a packet and say,

678
00:29:54,400 --> 00:29:56,720
okay, this packet is supposed to go that way.

679
00:29:56,720 --> 00:29:58,080
So, I'll send it that way,

680
00:29:58,080 --> 00:30:00,320
or this packet is supposed to go that way.

681
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:01,840
So, I'll send it that way.

682
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:04,000
So, the router, its entire job in life

683
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:06,400
is to sit there, receive your packets,

684
00:30:06,400 --> 00:30:09,760
and forward them to other post offices or hosts

685
00:30:09,760 --> 00:30:12,720
so that they get closer to their destination.

686
00:30:12,720 --> 00:30:14,800
So, sometimes we'll call them routers,

687
00:30:14,800 --> 00:30:16,320
sometimes we'll call them switches.

688
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:17,440
For the purpose of this class,

689
00:30:17,440 --> 00:30:19,040
they are basically the same.

690
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:21,920
Near the end, I'll tell you why there's two names for them,

691
00:30:21,920 --> 00:30:24,080
but for now, pretend they're the same thing.

692
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:28,240
So, we have end hosts, we have switches, aka routers.

693
00:30:29,440 --> 00:30:32,160
Okay, great.

694
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:34,560
So, as mentioned, when we built this thing,

695
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:36,640
we built it in layers.

696
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:37,760
I didn't call it out yet,

697
00:30:37,760 --> 00:30:40,640
but we actually built it in three separate layers.

698
00:30:40,640 --> 00:30:43,760
And what's great about this is we've created for ourselves

699
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:45,440
layers of abstraction.

700
00:30:45,440 --> 00:30:46,960
We love layers of abstraction.

701
00:30:46,960 --> 00:30:50,000
Hopefully, you've seen them in all the prerequisite classes,

702
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:51,840
61A, 61B.
61A，61B。

703
00:30:51,840 --> 00:30:53,600
Hopefully, you're sick of them at this point,

704
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:56,160
but layers of abstraction are wonderful.

705
00:30:56,160 --> 00:30:57,440
Layers of abstraction are why,

706
00:30:57,440 --> 00:30:59,440
when I'm programming on my computer,

707
00:30:59,440 --> 00:31:02,400
or when I'm using my computer to browse the web,

708
00:31:02,400 --> 00:31:05,200
I don't care about the voltage levels in my computer.

709
00:31:05,200 --> 00:31:06,560
I'm not thinking about that.

710
00:31:06,560 --> 00:31:09,440
Or when I drive a car, I'm not thinking about,

711
00:31:09,440 --> 00:31:11,920
oh, how is the fuel getting to the fuel tank?

712
00:31:11,920 --> 00:31:13,040
I don't care about that.

713
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:14,880
I just have to turn the steering wheel

714
00:31:14,880 --> 00:31:17,360
and the car goes the direction I want.

715
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:19,200
So layers of abstraction are great.

716
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:23,120
They allow me to hide lower level details that I don't need.

717
00:31:23,120 --> 00:31:24,800
And when we built the internet,

718
00:31:24,800 --> 00:31:27,440
we also built it with layers of abstraction.

719
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:30,240
So if you're someone working hard at layer three,

720
00:31:30,240 --> 00:31:33,520
that is your job is to connect up the local networks

721
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:34,720
to form the internet.

722
00:31:34,720 --> 00:31:37,280
For example, you're laying an undersea cable

723
00:31:37,280 --> 00:31:39,680
from like America to Europe.

724
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:41,760
You don't care about all the things happening beneath.

725
00:31:41,840 --> 00:31:44,000
You don't care about the voltage levels

726
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:46,160
that move bits across space on the wires.

727
00:31:46,160 --> 00:31:48,640
You don't care about how the local networks are linked up.

728
00:31:48,640 --> 00:31:51,040
You just care about building things at your layer,

729
00:31:51,040 --> 00:31:53,840
which is connecting up the local networks.

730
00:31:53,840 --> 00:31:55,280
Maybe another way of putting it is,

731
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:56,960
if you're the person building this link,

732
00:31:56,960 --> 00:32:00,240
you don't care what's happening inside this bubble, this town.

733
00:32:00,240 --> 00:32:02,080
You also don't care about what's happening in this town.

734
00:32:02,080 --> 00:32:03,920
You just have to drop this link.

735
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:04,560
That's your job.

736
00:32:05,200 --> 00:32:08,960
Or for example, if you're the person linking up hosts

737
00:32:08,960 --> 00:32:11,040
with links in a local network,

738
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:13,760
you don't care about the voltage levels in the wire.

739
00:32:13,760 --> 00:32:15,200
All you have to do is you say,

740
00:32:15,200 --> 00:32:18,480
there's this magical device that connects point A and point B.

741
00:32:18,480 --> 00:32:20,800
I'll use this to wire up a bunch of people.

742
00:32:20,800 --> 00:32:22,160
I have a network.

743
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:24,160
How the bits are moving across space?

744
00:32:24,160 --> 00:32:25,120
Is it optical?

745
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:26,640
Is it electrical?

746
00:32:26,640 --> 00:32:27,920
Not my concern.

747
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:29,520
So abstraction is really great.

748
00:32:29,520 --> 00:32:32,720
It allows us to innovate at these different layers separately.

749
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:34,400
We can have different teams,

750
00:32:34,400 --> 00:32:36,880
different researchers working at different layers,

751
00:32:36,880 --> 00:32:38,480
and they don't have to worry about

752
00:32:38,480 --> 00:32:40,240
what's happening at the other layers.

753
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:45,120
I can just assume that I'm given services from my lower layers

754
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:46,400
that I can rely on,

755
00:32:46,400 --> 00:32:50,480
and I provide services to the layer above me that they can use.

756
00:32:50,480 --> 00:32:51,200
So this is great.

757
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:53,120
We want to build the internet like this

758
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:55,760
because it gives us modularity.

759
00:32:55,760 --> 00:32:58,240
And that's a key idea that you'll see over and over again.

760
00:32:59,520 --> 00:33:02,080
Okay, as mentioned before,

761
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:04,320
because of the way we built the internet,

762
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:06,960
that is we use lots of post offices

763
00:33:06,960 --> 00:33:09,760
and connected everyone in the network of networks,

764
00:33:09,760 --> 00:33:12,080
what that means is that packets don't always

765
00:33:12,080 --> 00:33:14,160
directly reach their destination.

766
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:17,040
So for example, if this person wants to talk to this person,

767
00:33:17,040 --> 00:33:20,640
it is not the case that there is a direct wire connecting them.

768
00:33:20,640 --> 00:33:21,440
I don't know about you,

769
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:23,920
like there's no wire coming out of my computer

770
00:33:23,920 --> 00:33:26,000
that like goes all the way to Asia.

771
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:27,840
I don't think I have a wire like that.

772
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:28,880
So instead,

773
00:33:28,880 --> 00:33:31,440
if I want to send a packet somewhere else around the world,

774
00:33:31,440 --> 00:33:35,360
I have to forward the packet across multiple routers.

775
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:36,240
And in the analogy,

776
00:33:36,240 --> 00:33:39,200
this is like going between multiple post offices.

777
00:33:39,200 --> 00:33:41,600
So I send the packet to my local post office,

778
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:43,760
they send it to the post office next door,

779
00:33:43,760 --> 00:33:46,160
and then they put the mail like on a boat

780
00:33:46,160 --> 00:33:48,080
and the boat travels across the sea,

781
00:33:48,080 --> 00:33:49,520
goes to this post office,

782
00:33:49,520 --> 00:33:50,480
they forward it,

783
00:33:50,480 --> 00:33:51,520
crosses another sea,

784
00:33:51,520 --> 00:33:53,440
and then it goes to another post office.

785
00:33:53,440 --> 00:33:55,760
And only after all the hopping and hopping

786
00:33:55,760 --> 00:33:58,640
does it actually reach its final destination.

787
00:33:59,200 --> 00:34:02,880
So packets can take multiple hops is the term that we use

788
00:34:03,520 --> 00:34:05,120
before they can reach their destination.

789
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:06,640
And that's fine.

790
00:34:06,640 --> 00:34:08,639
That's just something we're going to use in our design,

791
00:34:08,639 --> 00:34:09,600
but that's how it's built.

792
00:34:11,440 --> 00:34:11,760
Okay.

793
00:34:13,040 --> 00:34:15,440
Also, because of the way that we built this,

794
00:34:15,440 --> 00:34:17,600
because we built it using layers,

795
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:20,080
we don't actually have to use the same technology

796
00:34:20,080 --> 00:34:21,360
at each of these,

797
00:34:21,360 --> 00:34:22,639
on each of these links.

798
00:34:22,639 --> 00:34:23,760
So for example,

799
00:34:23,760 --> 00:34:24,480
these links,

800
00:34:24,480 --> 00:34:25,520
they could be wired,

801
00:34:25,520 --> 00:34:28,320
like there's a wire connecting me to this post office.

802
00:34:28,320 --> 00:34:29,760
Maybe this link is optical.

803
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:31,280
That's a different technology.

804
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:32,000
That's okay.

805
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:34,480
The packet just travels across this link

806
00:34:34,480 --> 00:34:36,159
using a different technology.

807
00:34:36,159 --> 00:34:37,199
That's okay.

808
00:34:37,199 --> 00:34:38,800
And then maybe this link is wireless,

809
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:40,560
like it's using radio waves.

810
00:34:40,560 --> 00:34:42,400
And then maybe this link is wired again.

811
00:34:42,400 --> 00:34:43,760
And these links are optical.

812
00:34:43,760 --> 00:34:45,120
That's totally fine.

813
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:48,400
Everyone can use their own link level technology.

814
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:51,440
And the packet still reaches its destination.

815
00:34:51,440 --> 00:34:52,480
So this is wonderful.

816
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:54,639
This allows different networks

817
00:34:54,639 --> 00:34:56,800
to use the technology that's best for them.

818
00:34:56,800 --> 00:34:58,720
So maybe this network decided,

819
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:00,000
I want to use wireless,

820
00:35:00,000 --> 00:35:01,760
but this one thinks I want to use optical.

821
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:02,720
It's totally fine.

822
00:35:02,720 --> 00:35:03,840
They can do that.

823
00:35:03,840 --> 00:35:05,920
And everything works out nicely.

824
00:35:06,480 --> 00:35:07,680
So this is great.

825
00:35:07,680 --> 00:35:09,760
And it's because of the layers of abstraction

826
00:35:09,760 --> 00:35:10,880
that we got this to work.

827
00:35:11,840 --> 00:35:12,960
It's because at layer three,

828
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:14,320
we don't care what's going on below.

829
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:15,760
Okay.

830
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:19,040
So one more property of layer three

831
00:35:19,040 --> 00:35:20,160
that we should talk about.

832
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:21,600
And if you're wondering why it's like this,

833
00:35:21,600 --> 00:35:23,280
we'll talk about why next time.

834
00:35:23,280 --> 00:35:25,200
Today is more about how it's built.

835
00:35:25,200 --> 00:35:27,440
Next time is why we built it this way.

836
00:35:27,440 --> 00:35:29,120
But layer three service model

837
00:35:29,120 --> 00:35:31,680
is something we call best effort.

838
00:35:31,680 --> 00:35:34,320
Best effort basically means I will try,

839
00:35:34,400 --> 00:35:37,040
but if I don't succeed, it sucks to be you.

840
00:35:37,040 --> 00:35:39,200
I'm not going to promise that it's going to work.

841
00:35:39,200 --> 00:35:41,840
So what this means is a couple of things.

842
00:35:41,840 --> 00:35:44,000
One, it means that packets are limited in size.

843
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:45,600
That's not really best effort,

844
00:35:45,600 --> 00:35:47,200
but I couldn't fit it anywhere else.

845
00:35:47,200 --> 00:35:49,840
But just be aware that packets can't be infinitely large.

846
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:50,960
There's limit to them.

847
00:35:50,960 --> 00:35:52,000
Now you know.

848
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:54,000
But here's the part that is best effort.

849
00:35:54,000 --> 00:35:55,920
So packets can get lost.

850
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:57,440
For example, I don't know,

851
00:35:57,440 --> 00:35:58,880
this post office gets confused

852
00:35:58,880 --> 00:36:00,160
and sends it the wrong way.

853
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:03,440
Packet gets lost, doesn't reach its destination.

854
00:36:03,440 --> 00:36:05,040
Packets could get reordered.

855
00:36:05,040 --> 00:36:07,760
Like I send two packets, part one and part two,

856
00:36:07,760 --> 00:36:09,760
and part two gets there before part one.

857
00:36:09,760 --> 00:36:10,960
That could happen.

858
00:36:10,960 --> 00:36:12,640
Packets could get corrupted.

859
00:36:12,640 --> 00:36:15,600
The data inside could actually become incorrect

860
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:18,480
as it travels across the network.

861
00:36:18,480 --> 00:36:19,840
So all of these are failures

862
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:21,520
that can happen on the network.

863
00:36:21,520 --> 00:36:23,840
And what the network is basically telling you is,

864
00:36:23,840 --> 00:36:26,800
I will try to get your packet toward its destination,

865
00:36:26,800 --> 00:36:28,560
but I'm not going to promise.

866
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:31,120
If I fail, it sucks to be you.

867
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:32,720
I'm not going to fix it for you.

868
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:35,680
And in fact, the network won't even tell you if it fails.

869
00:36:35,680 --> 00:36:37,280
So you won't even get an error message

870
00:36:37,280 --> 00:36:39,360
saying that the delivery failed.

871
00:36:39,360 --> 00:36:41,840
You just have to deal with the failure yourself.

872
00:36:41,840 --> 00:36:43,920
So that's the best effort service model

873
00:36:43,920 --> 00:36:46,000
and that is what the network provides.

874
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:47,440
And again, if you think this is weird

875
00:36:47,440 --> 00:36:49,520
and you want to pick a different service model,

876
00:36:49,520 --> 00:36:50,720
you come back next time.

877
00:36:50,720 --> 00:36:53,280
We'll talk about why it was designed this way.

878
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:55,040
But basically the punchline here is,

879
00:36:55,040 --> 00:36:56,960
we can't stop at layer three.

880
00:36:56,960 --> 00:36:58,720
If we stop at layer three,

881
00:36:58,720 --> 00:37:01,280
every piece of data you ever send or receive

882
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:02,160
is best effort.

883
00:37:02,160 --> 00:37:04,080
You send it, it doesn't get there.

884
00:37:04,080 --> 00:37:06,160
Sorry, nothing I can do about it.

885
00:37:06,160 --> 00:37:09,440
If you care about things actually reaching their destination

886
00:37:09,440 --> 00:37:13,120
with a guarantee on them, we have to build more layers.

887
00:37:13,120 --> 00:37:15,040
So we're going to build more layers.

888
00:37:16,720 --> 00:37:18,480
Unless you have questions before I build more,

889
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:20,400
but we're done with one, two, three for now.

890
00:37:21,200 --> 00:37:22,640
And we're going to go further up.

891
00:37:22,640 --> 00:37:22,960
Question.

892
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:30,960
That's a great question.

893
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:33,600
How does the router know where the destination is?

894
00:37:33,600 --> 00:37:35,360
Like, do I forward it that way or that way?

895
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:37,520
It's going to take me six lectures to answer that question.

896
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:39,600
So come back, I don't know, in a couple of weeks.

897
00:37:39,600 --> 00:37:41,440
We're going to spend six lectures answering that.

898
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:42,240
But it's a great question.

899
00:37:43,520 --> 00:37:45,120
Okay, there's a whole topic called routing

900
00:37:45,120 --> 00:37:45,920
that's going to solve that.

901
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:49,040
Any other great questions?

902
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:50,960
Okay, great.

903
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:53,600
So we finished building layers one, two, three.

904
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:56,320
We're now going to build four through seven.

905
00:37:56,320 --> 00:37:58,240
There's a new layer that we're going to build.

906
00:37:58,240 --> 00:38:00,560
And remember, the reason why we're building this layer

907
00:38:00,560 --> 00:38:03,360
is because if we stopped at layer three, it's true.

908
00:38:03,360 --> 00:38:05,040
We get global connectivity.

909
00:38:05,040 --> 00:38:07,120
I can send packets anywhere in the world,

910
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:10,080
but there's no guarantee that they actually arrive,

911
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:11,280
which I don't like.

912
00:38:11,280 --> 00:38:13,520
I want packets to actually get there.

913
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:16,800
So to make sure that packets actually reach their destination,

914
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:18,400
I introduce a layer four.

915
00:38:19,040 --> 00:38:21,200
Layer four builds on top of layer three,

916
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:25,120
which means I can use the services provided by layer three.

917
00:38:25,120 --> 00:38:27,280
That is global connectivity.

918
00:38:27,280 --> 00:38:30,320
But I'm now going to add extra features

919
00:38:30,320 --> 00:38:32,320
to make sure that packets actually get there.

920
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:34,720
For example, if a packet gets lost,

921
00:38:34,720 --> 00:38:37,200
I'm going to send it again and send it again

922
00:38:37,200 --> 00:38:39,200
and send it again until it gets there.

923
00:38:39,200 --> 00:38:41,040
If a packet arrives out of order,

924
00:38:41,040 --> 00:38:44,320
I'm going to reassemble them in the correct order.

925
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:47,120
If the user wants to send a big piece of data,

926
00:38:47,120 --> 00:38:50,080
I'll split the data up and then reassemble it

927
00:38:50,080 --> 00:38:52,480
into the form that the user wants.

928
00:38:52,480 --> 00:38:55,840
So all of these features and more happen at layer four,

929
00:38:55,840 --> 00:38:57,280
which we call transport.

930
00:38:57,280 --> 00:39:00,400
And this allows us to reliably deliver packets.

931
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:04,320
And when we transition from layer three to four,

932
00:39:04,320 --> 00:39:07,600
I think there's also a little bit of a change in the mindset

933
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:09,200
in which we think about things.

934
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:10,640
These are not official terms,

935
00:39:10,640 --> 00:39:13,760
but what I think about when I think about layers one, two, three

936
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:16,240
is I think about like a packet mindset.

937
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:18,640
And what that means is each piece of data,

938
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:21,360
it's a packet and that packet is independent.

939
00:39:21,360 --> 00:39:26,000
So the internet is just all these packets flying around independently.

940
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:28,640
There's nothing that connects one packet to the other.

941
00:39:28,640 --> 00:39:31,600
They're all just separate packets flying across the internet.

942
00:39:31,600 --> 00:39:33,360
But once I get to layer four,

943
00:39:33,360 --> 00:39:35,760
I can stop thinking in terms of packets,

944
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:39,280
which is just pieces of data flying around the internet.

945
00:39:39,280 --> 00:39:43,920
I can start thinking instead in terms of flows or connections.

946
00:39:43,920 --> 00:39:46,320
And what that means is this is actually a stream

947
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:50,160
of related packets being exchanged between two people.

948
00:39:50,160 --> 00:39:52,480
So oftentimes when you want to talk to someone,

949
00:39:52,480 --> 00:39:54,080
you're talking to someone for a long time.

950
00:39:54,080 --> 00:39:58,320
We have a conversation or I'm downloading a video from YouTube.

951
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:01,840
Well, in that case, I am talking to YouTube for a very long time.

952
00:40:01,840 --> 00:40:05,520
And so we're going to be exchanging lots of related packets.

953
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:07,280
Like here's the first part of your video.

954
00:40:07,280 --> 00:40:08,640
Here's the next part of your video.

955
00:40:08,640 --> 00:40:11,280
Here's the next part of your video and so forth.

956
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:12,800
So once you get to layer four,

957
00:40:12,800 --> 00:40:15,680
we stop thinking in terms of individual packets

958
00:40:15,680 --> 00:40:17,200
floating across the internet.

959
00:40:17,200 --> 00:40:19,440
And we start thinking in terms of flows,

960
00:40:19,440 --> 00:40:22,800
a bunch of related packets streaming back and forth

961
00:40:22,800 --> 00:40:26,720
between a sender and a receiver, a client and a server,

962
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:29,440
point A and point B, basically two people.

963
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:31,680
So once you get to layer four and above,

964
00:40:31,680 --> 00:40:34,320
we can start thinking in a slightly different mindset.

965
00:40:34,320 --> 00:40:36,000
And we'll think in both of these mindsets

966
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:37,360
plenty throughout the semester.

967
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:40,640
So it's okay if you don't totally see the difference yet,

968
00:40:40,640 --> 00:40:42,880
but something I wanted to call out early.

969
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:46,720
Okay, so we have a final layer.

970
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:48,720
By the way, if you're annoyed by the counting here,

971
00:40:48,720 --> 00:40:50,720
it's because five and six are obsolete.

972
00:40:50,720 --> 00:40:53,120
This model was developed in like the 1970s

973
00:40:53,120 --> 00:40:55,600
and five and six are no longer used.

974
00:40:55,600 --> 00:40:57,680
So instead we have to skip from four to seven.

975
00:40:57,680 --> 00:40:58,720
Everyone does it.

976
00:40:58,720 --> 00:40:59,760
We'll deal with it.

977
00:40:59,760 --> 00:41:03,200
But basically the idea is that now you have layer four

978
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:06,400
and that unlocks the ability for users to build

979
00:41:06,400 --> 00:41:10,240
whatever applications they want on top of layer four.

980
00:41:10,240 --> 00:41:12,320
So for example, you're building YouTube.

981
00:41:12,320 --> 00:41:14,000
You want to give videos to the user?

982
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:14,880
That's great.

983
00:41:14,880 --> 00:41:18,400
You can use the transport layer to form connections

984
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:20,640
and send videos to users.

985
00:41:21,760 --> 00:41:23,840
What if instead, I don't want to build a video service.

986
00:41:23,840 --> 00:41:26,080
I want to build a gaming service

987
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:28,560
or I want to build the game that runs online.

988
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:29,120
That's great.

989
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:31,440
In that case, your application is a game

990
00:41:31,440 --> 00:41:33,360
and it still runs on top of layer four.

991
00:41:33,360 --> 00:41:35,600
It sends data back and forth between users.

992
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:37,920
Or you could build email.

993
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:38,720
Okay, that's great.

994
00:41:38,720 --> 00:41:41,600
You build email, it still runs on top of layer four.

995
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:44,960
So what's great about this layering abstraction

996
00:41:44,960 --> 00:41:46,720
or this layering structure

997
00:41:46,720 --> 00:41:48,880
is that once we have these four layers,

998
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:51,040
users can build whatever the heck they want

999
00:41:51,040 --> 00:41:52,800
on top of the four layers.

1000
00:41:52,800 --> 00:41:54,880
They get reliable connectivity

1001
00:41:54,880 --> 00:41:57,440
which relies on global connectivity

1002
00:41:57,440 --> 00:41:59,520
which relies on individual links

1003
00:41:59,520 --> 00:42:02,720
which relies on physical data floating across space.

1004
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:05,680
But as the programmer, you don't care about any of this stuff.

1005
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:07,360
You can just build on top of it

1006
00:42:07,360 --> 00:42:10,080
and say, I want to build gaming application.

1007
00:42:10,080 --> 00:42:11,440
Okay, great, build it.

1008
00:42:11,440 --> 00:42:13,920
And you can build it on top of the internet.

1009
00:42:13,920 --> 00:42:16,400
So when users are building things using the internet,

1010
00:42:16,400 --> 00:42:18,880
they're building up here at layer seven.

1011
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:20,080
And that's something we'll look at too.

1012
00:42:21,760 --> 00:42:23,600
Those are the five layers that we care about.

1013
00:42:24,480 --> 00:42:26,240
There's going to be little asterisks

1014
00:42:26,240 --> 00:42:28,400
and little additions throughout the semester.

1015
00:42:28,400 --> 00:42:30,880
But that's kind of the general model that we use.

1016
00:42:30,880 --> 00:42:32,400
I guess I should also mention at this point,

1017
00:42:32,400 --> 00:42:34,400
I don't think I called it out anywhere else,

1018
00:42:34,400 --> 00:42:36,800
that this is not a perfect picture.

1019
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:38,160
So it's not always going to be the case

1020
00:42:38,160 --> 00:42:41,360
that something cleanly slots into a given layer.

1021
00:42:41,360 --> 00:42:44,320
So there are protocols that span different layers.

1022
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:47,520
There are protocols that kind of live between layers.

1023
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:49,680
So this is a nice conceptual picture,

1024
00:42:49,680 --> 00:42:51,600
but just want to call out it's not the case

1025
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:54,080
that everything fits cleanly in these layers.

1026
00:42:54,080 --> 00:42:57,040
This is more of a design principle that we use

1027
00:42:57,040 --> 00:42:58,160
to make things look good.

1028
00:42:58,720 --> 00:43:02,080
But in real life, the picture is a little bit messier.

1029
00:43:02,080 --> 00:43:03,280
But this is kind of what it looks like.

1030
00:43:05,920 --> 00:43:08,720
Okay, thoughts, questions.

1031
00:43:08,720 --> 00:43:10,640
Do you want like a one minute break?

1032
00:43:10,640 --> 00:43:11,600
I know it's late at night.

1033
00:43:11,600 --> 00:43:14,400
So it's dead silent.

1034
00:43:14,400 --> 00:43:15,760
I can't tell if that's a yes or no.

1035
00:43:15,760 --> 00:43:17,040
You want a one minute break?

1036
00:43:17,840 --> 00:43:19,600
You want me to just plow on and finish this thing?

1037
00:43:20,720 --> 00:43:22,000
I'll give you like one minute.

1038
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:23,600
Okay, enjoy and then we'll finish.

1039
00:43:41,600 --> 00:44:07,600
All right, I can take Zoom questions while I'm waiting.

1040
00:44:11,600 --> 00:44:21,600
All right, this is Zoom questions I've been answered.

1041
00:44:21,600 --> 00:44:23,600
Thanks.

1042
00:44:41,920 --> 00:45:02,640
For the layers one, I think five is session and six is presentation.

1043
00:45:03,360 --> 00:45:07,840
And they had to do with like how the application looks.

1044
00:45:08,720 --> 00:45:10,640
But beyond that, I truly don't know anything

1045
00:45:10,640 --> 00:45:11,520
about those two layers.

1046
