1047
00:45:23,200 --> 00:45:24,640
These are all good questions though.

1048
00:45:40,800 --> 00:45:58,800
Okay, is everyone ready to keep going?

1049
00:45:59,760 --> 00:46:04,480
Five, four, three, two, one.

1050
00:46:04,480 --> 00:46:07,520
Okay, time to keep going.

1051
00:46:07,520 --> 00:46:09,760
So there's just one more topic for today

1052
00:46:09,760 --> 00:46:10,960
and we are all out of here.

1053
00:46:10,960 --> 00:46:15,760
Okay, so let's think about what we've built so far.

1054
00:46:15,760 --> 00:46:18,560
We've built something that gives you global connectivity

1055
00:46:18,560 --> 00:46:20,160
using all these different layers.

1056
00:46:20,160 --> 00:46:23,760
And remember that when you send data, you send it in a packet.

1057
00:46:23,760 --> 00:46:27,360
That's a bunch of ones and zeros representing the data you want to send.

1058
00:46:27,360 --> 00:46:30,000
And the packet travels across multiple hops

1059
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:32,240
until it reaches its destination.

1060
00:46:32,240 --> 00:46:33,840
But there's just one problem.

1061
00:46:33,840 --> 00:46:35,840
If I take all these ones and zeros

1062
00:46:35,840 --> 00:46:38,160
and I just deliver them to my post office,

1063
00:46:38,160 --> 00:46:41,040
this post office has no idea what you're talking about.

1064
00:46:41,040 --> 00:46:42,800
What are these ones and zeros?

1065
00:46:42,800 --> 00:46:44,720
What am I supposed to do with them?

1066
00:46:44,720 --> 00:46:46,560
It's like if I go to my post office

1067
00:46:46,560 --> 00:46:49,280
and I just hand them a box, okay, great.

1068
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:50,800
What do I do with the box?

1069
00:46:50,800 --> 00:46:54,560
So we actually have to add some extra information

1070
00:46:54,560 --> 00:46:56,160
which we'll call metadata.

1071
00:46:56,160 --> 00:46:59,440
And that's going to tell us what to do with the box.

1072
00:46:59,440 --> 00:47:02,400
So in the analogy, you cannot go to the post office

1073
00:47:02,400 --> 00:47:04,320
and just give them a piece of paper and say,

1074
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:06,800
here you go, you have to put the piece of paper

1075
00:47:06,800 --> 00:47:08,160
in an envelope.

1076
00:47:08,160 --> 00:47:11,520
And on the envelope, you have to write information about

1077
00:47:11,520 --> 00:47:13,440
where do you want this letter to go?

1078
00:47:13,440 --> 00:47:14,800
Who is sending the letter?

1079
00:47:14,800 --> 00:47:16,000
Things like that.

1080
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:18,960
So instead of just sending a bunch of ones and zeros

1081
00:47:18,960 --> 00:47:20,560
that correspond to my data,

1082
00:47:20,560 --> 00:47:23,200
I need to put extra information on top

1083
00:47:23,200 --> 00:47:26,400
that tells the routers what to do with the letter

1084
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:28,720
so that they can take my letter or my packet

1085
00:47:28,720 --> 00:47:31,840
and deliver it to its correct destination.

1086
00:47:31,840 --> 00:47:34,080
So this is the motivation for why we need headers.

1087
00:47:34,800 --> 00:47:36,800
So here's your very first header.

1088
00:47:36,800 --> 00:47:39,520
Headers can have all sorts of interesting data in them.

1089
00:47:39,520 --> 00:47:40,640
A lot of modern protocols,

1090
00:47:40,640 --> 00:47:43,920
we'll see protocols with like 10 things in the header.

1091
00:47:43,920 --> 00:47:45,920
But for today, let's just think about

1092
00:47:45,920 --> 00:47:49,120
the two simplest things you could possibly put in a header.

1093
00:47:49,120 --> 00:47:51,840
So one thing that the header clearly needs is,

1094
00:47:51,840 --> 00:47:54,560
we have to put where the packet is going.

1095
00:47:54,560 --> 00:47:56,640
If I don't put it's going to be,

1096
00:47:56,640 --> 00:47:59,200
these routers are simply going to have no idea

1097
00:47:59,200 --> 00:48:01,680
where this packet is supposed to go.

1098
00:48:01,680 --> 00:48:03,360
So I have to put in the header,

1099
00:48:03,360 --> 00:48:06,320
this packet is destined for B, that's required.

1100
00:48:06,320 --> 00:48:07,360
If you don't put this,

1101
00:48:07,360 --> 00:48:09,200
the routers have no idea where it's going.

1102
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:13,520
Another useful header that's technically not required

1103
00:48:13,520 --> 00:48:15,520
but is basically always there

1104
00:48:15,520 --> 00:48:18,800
because it's useful is who's sending the packet.

1105
00:48:18,800 --> 00:48:19,680
So for example here,

1106
00:48:19,680 --> 00:48:22,160
I would say the packet is coming from A.

1107
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:23,680
And this is useful for example,

1108
00:48:23,680 --> 00:48:26,560
if the recipient B wants to reply,

1109
00:48:26,560 --> 00:48:28,800
they can look at the letter or the envelope

1110
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:30,000
or the header and say,

1111
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:32,000
oh, this packet was from A,

1112
00:48:32,080 --> 00:48:33,920
I'm going to send a reply to A.

1113
00:48:34,640 --> 00:48:36,640
So while not required,

1114
00:48:36,640 --> 00:48:39,360
having the sender field is very useful,

1115
00:48:39,360 --> 00:48:40,720
especially if for example,

1116
00:48:40,720 --> 00:48:42,880
the recipient wants to send a reply.

1117
00:48:42,880 --> 00:48:45,360
So in basically all headers we'll see,

1118
00:48:45,360 --> 00:48:47,440
there's going to be a from field and a to field,

1119
00:48:48,160 --> 00:48:50,800
two things that are very useful in headers.

1120
00:48:51,360 --> 00:48:53,200
And so these are called the headers,

1121
00:48:53,200 --> 00:48:54,320
they're the metadata,

1122
00:48:54,320 --> 00:48:56,160
the thing you write on top of the envelope,

1123
00:48:56,160 --> 00:48:58,240
and the thing inside the actual data,

1124
00:48:58,240 --> 00:48:59,440
that's the payload.

1125
00:48:59,440 --> 00:49:00,880
Just a fancy way of saying,

1126
00:49:00,880 --> 00:49:02,800
that's the actual data you want to send.

1127
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:04,960
These ones and zeros could represent an image,

1128
00:49:04,960 --> 00:49:06,560
a video, whatever you want.

1129
00:49:07,440 --> 00:49:10,320
Okay, something important about this header is,

1130
00:49:10,960 --> 00:49:12,480
it's not actually in English.

1131
00:49:12,480 --> 00:49:13,840
Like if I open up my computer,

1132
00:49:13,840 --> 00:49:15,600
it doesn't say from A to B,

1133
00:49:15,600 --> 00:49:16,160
that'd be nice,

1134
00:49:16,160 --> 00:49:17,520
but that's not what it says.

1135
00:49:17,520 --> 00:49:21,040
This is actually encoded in lots of ones and zeros as well.

1136
00:49:21,040 --> 00:49:22,400
And as we mentioned before,

1137
00:49:22,400 --> 00:49:24,000
when we design a protocol,

1138
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:26,800
everyone has to agree on what the protocol looks like.

1139
00:49:26,800 --> 00:49:28,640
So what that means is for example,

1140
00:49:29,360 --> 00:49:30,640
when you design this protocol,

1141
00:49:30,720 --> 00:49:32,400
you might say, first eight bits,

1142
00:49:32,400 --> 00:49:34,080
tell me who the packet is from.

1143
00:49:34,080 --> 00:49:35,200
And the next eight bits,

1144
00:49:35,200 --> 00:49:36,800
tell me where the packet is going to,

1145
00:49:36,800 --> 00:49:37,760
things like that.

1146
00:49:37,760 --> 00:49:39,760
But everyone has to agree on this.

1147
00:49:39,760 --> 00:49:41,200
If you're using one format,

1148
00:49:41,200 --> 00:49:42,800
and I'm using a different format,

1149
00:49:42,800 --> 00:49:44,480
we don't speak the same language.

1150
00:49:44,480 --> 00:49:45,920
So when you send a packet,

1151
00:49:45,920 --> 00:49:48,880
I'm going to have no idea what your header is saying.

1152
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:50,560
So when we design these headers,

1153
00:49:50,560 --> 00:49:53,040
they have to be designed in a standardized way,

1154
00:49:53,040 --> 00:49:54,240
and all computers,

1155
00:49:54,240 --> 00:49:55,520
my computer, your computer,

1156
00:49:56,320 --> 00:49:57,600
every other computer,

1157
00:49:57,600 --> 00:49:59,360
they all understand exactly

1158
00:49:59,360 --> 00:50:00,800
what these headers look like.

1159
00:50:00,800 --> 00:50:02,800
It's very important to make sure that

1160
00:50:03,520 --> 00:50:05,360
the packets make sense to everyone.

1161
00:50:06,320 --> 00:50:08,480
Okay, so those are what headers look like.

1162
00:50:08,480 --> 00:50:09,840
Now we know why they're needed.

1163
00:50:09,840 --> 00:50:11,920
And I'm going to draw you a picture of how headers

1164
00:50:12,480 --> 00:50:14,240
relate to the layers of abstraction

1165
00:50:14,240 --> 00:50:15,200
that we talk about.

1166
00:50:15,200 --> 00:50:17,440
So we'll combine these two topics together

1167
00:50:17,440 --> 00:50:20,160
to finish our kind of high level overview

1168
00:50:20,160 --> 00:50:21,440
of what the internet looks like.

1169
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:26,000
So here's kind of another mail analogy picture.

1170
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:26,880
So at the very bottom,

1171
00:50:26,880 --> 00:50:28,160
we have the mail room.

1172
00:50:28,160 --> 00:50:31,360
Those are the rooms that move mail from point A to point B.

1173
00:50:31,360 --> 00:50:33,200
And then maybe we have a secretary.

1174
00:50:33,200 --> 00:50:34,080
And then at the very top,

1175
00:50:34,080 --> 00:50:35,760
we have the boss, the CEO.

1176
00:50:36,400 --> 00:50:38,000
So let's say, here's Alice.

1177
00:50:38,640 --> 00:50:39,760
She's the CEO.

1178
00:50:39,760 --> 00:50:41,280
She wants to send a message to Bob,

1179
00:50:41,280 --> 00:50:42,480
who's also the CEO.

1180
00:50:42,480 --> 00:50:44,400
And she has this very ominous message.

1181
00:50:44,400 --> 00:50:45,680
I don't know why it's so ominous,

1182
00:50:45,680 --> 00:50:47,120
but they were in the oldest lights,

1183
00:50:47,120 --> 00:50:48,400
so I copied them over.

1184
00:50:48,400 --> 00:50:49,440
So Alice wants to say,

1185
00:50:49,440 --> 00:50:50,560
your days are numbered.

1186
00:50:50,560 --> 00:50:51,600
Very spooky.

1187
00:50:51,600 --> 00:50:53,040
And she wants to send this to Bob.

1188
00:50:53,600 --> 00:50:54,240
And remember,

1189
00:50:54,240 --> 00:50:57,120
the way that we built the layers of abstraction

1190
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:00,640
is that I guess you'll see once we do the demo,

1191
00:51:00,640 --> 00:51:03,360
but the packet's actually going to travel down the stack

1192
00:51:03,360 --> 00:51:04,240
and then back up.

1193
00:51:04,240 --> 00:51:05,760
So let's see what that looks like.

1194
00:51:05,760 --> 00:51:07,040
So Alice is the CEO.

1195
00:51:07,040 --> 00:51:08,160
She's the boss.

1196
00:51:08,160 --> 00:51:11,360
She has no time for petty matters like sending mail.

1197
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:12,560
So what's she going to do?

1198
00:51:12,560 --> 00:51:15,040
She's going to talk to her secretary and say,

1199
00:51:15,040 --> 00:51:17,920
secretary, I have mail that I want to send.

1200
00:51:17,920 --> 00:51:19,600
I am the rich CEO.

1201
00:51:19,600 --> 00:51:21,280
I'm going to make you send it for me.

1202
00:51:21,280 --> 00:51:22,720
So she takes her letter,

1203
00:51:22,720 --> 00:51:24,560
hands it off to the secretary,

1204
00:51:24,560 --> 00:51:26,480
and the secretary does the work of

1205
00:51:26,560 --> 00:51:28,640
putting that letter in the envelope

1206
00:51:28,640 --> 00:51:30,080
and attaching a header.

1207
00:51:30,080 --> 00:51:33,360
And the header says it's from Alice and it goes to Bob.

1208
00:51:33,360 --> 00:51:35,280
So the secretary did some extra work

1209
00:51:35,280 --> 00:51:38,720
and the secretary wrapped a header around the packet.

1210
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:40,480
That's something that we're going to see.

1211
00:51:41,200 --> 00:51:43,520
So an extra header got attached

1212
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:47,040
as Alice passed the letter down to the secretary.

1213
00:51:47,840 --> 00:51:48,880
And now the secretary says,

1214
00:51:48,880 --> 00:51:49,920
well, I'm the secretary,

1215
00:51:49,920 --> 00:51:52,240
but my job is in sending mail.

1216
00:51:52,240 --> 00:51:53,440
I'm not a mailman.

1217
00:51:53,440 --> 00:51:56,240
So I'm going to pass this downstairs one more time

1218
00:51:56,240 --> 00:51:57,520
to the mailroom.

1219
00:51:57,520 --> 00:51:59,040
And the mailroom is going to look at this envelope

1220
00:51:59,040 --> 00:52:02,240
and say, okay, well, this is from Alice's building

1221
00:52:02,240 --> 00:52:04,000
and it's going to Bob's building.

1222
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:06,160
So I'm going to attach another header

1223
00:52:06,160 --> 00:52:08,720
and this header has Alice's street address

1224
00:52:08,720 --> 00:52:10,480
and Bob's street address.

1225
00:52:10,480 --> 00:52:12,320
And I'm going to attach this other header

1226
00:52:12,320 --> 00:52:13,200
and in the analogy,

1227
00:52:13,200 --> 00:52:16,160
you can think of this as putting the envelope in a box.

1228
00:52:16,160 --> 00:52:18,640
So now we have a letter wrapped in an envelope,

1229
00:52:18,640 --> 00:52:21,600
wrapped in a box, two layers of wrapping around it.

1230
00:52:22,160 --> 00:52:24,160
And now that we've reached the very bottom,

1231
00:52:24,160 --> 00:52:26,560
this box can go into a mail truck

1232
00:52:26,560 --> 00:52:28,240
or a mailman can carry it

1233
00:52:28,240 --> 00:52:30,480
all the way over to Bob's building.

1234
00:52:31,440 --> 00:52:33,200
So what happens at Bob's building?

1235
00:52:33,200 --> 00:52:35,920
Well, the mailroom opens up the box.

1236
00:52:35,920 --> 00:52:38,240
Though I got mail, I open up the box.

1237
00:52:38,240 --> 00:52:41,040
And so what happens is this outer most layer,

1238
00:52:41,040 --> 00:52:43,280
the box gets unwrapped, right?

1239
00:52:43,280 --> 00:52:44,160
I don't need this anymore.

1240
00:52:44,160 --> 00:52:46,480
It's already at four, five, six Bob's street.

1241
00:52:46,480 --> 00:52:48,160
It's reached Bob's building.

1242
00:52:48,160 --> 00:52:50,400
I don't need this outer header anymore.

1243
00:52:50,400 --> 00:52:52,080
So I'm going to unwrap it.

1244
00:52:52,640 --> 00:52:53,920
And when I unwrap it,

1245
00:52:53,920 --> 00:52:56,640
I expose this next header that says,

1246
00:52:56,640 --> 00:52:58,080
oh, it's going to Bob.

1247
00:52:58,080 --> 00:53:00,640
So I'm going to go ahead and give this to Bob's secretary.

1248
00:53:00,640 --> 00:53:03,840
So notice what happened here is that the box got unwrapped.

1249
00:53:03,840 --> 00:53:06,320
It reveals the inner header

1250
00:53:06,320 --> 00:53:09,040
and I pass the envelope up to the secretary.

1251
00:53:09,600 --> 00:53:10,720
And what does the secretary do?

1252
00:53:10,720 --> 00:53:13,040
What the secretary says, I got an envelope.

1253
00:53:13,040 --> 00:53:15,040
I'm going to open it and see what's inside.

1254
00:53:15,040 --> 00:53:18,480
So the secretary is going to unwrap another header,

1255
00:53:18,480 --> 00:53:20,400
specifically this blue one,

1256
00:53:20,400 --> 00:53:22,400
exposing the original message.

1257
00:53:22,400 --> 00:53:25,280
And then the secretary passes the letter up to Bob

1258
00:53:25,280 --> 00:53:27,040
and Bob gets the original message

1259
00:53:27,040 --> 00:53:28,800
with no extra headers attached.

1260
00:53:29,600 --> 00:53:30,800
So to play it one more time,

1261
00:53:31,360 --> 00:53:32,240
we started up here.

1262
00:53:32,240 --> 00:53:33,520
Alice had a message.

1263
00:53:33,520 --> 00:53:35,760
And as we went down the stack

1264
00:53:35,760 --> 00:53:38,160
from higher layers to lower layers,

1265
00:53:38,160 --> 00:53:40,240
more headers got wrapped around.

1266
00:53:40,240 --> 00:53:42,960
The secretary wrapped an envelope around the letter.

1267
00:53:42,960 --> 00:53:46,240
The mailman wrapped a box around the envelope.

1268
00:53:46,240 --> 00:53:47,680
Once we got to the bottom,

1269
00:53:47,680 --> 00:53:51,440
it was sent across the mail system to Bob's building.

1270
00:53:51,440 --> 00:53:55,360
And then Bob's building unwrapped the headers one by one

1271
00:53:55,360 --> 00:53:57,440
as the packet went upwards.

1272
00:53:57,440 --> 00:53:58,320
So I started here.

1273
00:53:59,040 --> 00:54:00,720
We lost the outer header.

1274
00:54:00,720 --> 00:54:02,480
It exposes the inner header,

1275
00:54:02,480 --> 00:54:04,080
pass it up to the secretary.

1276
00:54:04,080 --> 00:54:06,160
The secretary unwraps the envelope,

1277
00:54:06,160 --> 00:54:07,600
exposes the letter,

1278
00:54:07,600 --> 00:54:09,280
and then gives the letter to Bob.

1279
00:54:09,280 --> 00:54:10,720
So that's the pattern that we're going to see

1280
00:54:10,720 --> 00:54:12,080
over and over again.

1281
00:54:12,080 --> 00:54:14,080
The messages start at the very top.

1282
00:54:14,080 --> 00:54:15,360
They work their way down,

1283
00:54:15,360 --> 00:54:17,840
getting more layers added to them

1284
00:54:17,840 --> 00:54:19,520
and more headers added to them,

1285
00:54:19,520 --> 00:54:20,720
like an onion.

1286
00:54:20,720 --> 00:54:21,920
And then on the other side,

1287
00:54:21,920 --> 00:54:23,280
the onion gets unwrapped,

1288
00:54:23,280 --> 00:54:25,680
exposing the original message inside.

1289
00:54:25,680 --> 00:54:27,520
And this happens across all the layers.

1290
00:54:29,360 --> 00:54:31,280
Thoughts on the analogy so far?

1291
00:54:32,080 --> 00:54:32,400
Okay.

1292
00:54:33,120 --> 00:54:34,400
So that's the postal analogy.

1293
00:54:34,400 --> 00:54:35,280
So to show it one more time,

1294
00:54:35,280 --> 00:54:36,720
I guess I forgot I had this slide.

1295
00:54:36,720 --> 00:54:37,920
So as I move down,

1296
00:54:37,920 --> 00:54:40,720
you can see that more headers get wrapped.

1297
00:54:40,720 --> 00:54:42,160
And as I move back up,

1298
00:54:42,160 --> 00:54:43,680
the headers get unwrapped.

1299
00:54:43,680 --> 00:54:44,720
So that's how it works.

1300
00:54:45,440 --> 00:54:45,760
Okay.

1301
00:54:46,480 --> 00:54:48,240
So something interesting about this

1302
00:54:48,240 --> 00:54:50,000
is if you look at these headers,

1303
00:54:50,080 --> 00:54:51,440
you can almost think of the header

1304
00:54:51,440 --> 00:54:54,080
as talking to your equivalent

1305
00:54:54,080 --> 00:54:55,680
on the other side.

1306
00:54:55,680 --> 00:54:57,120
So what I mean by that is,

1307
00:54:57,120 --> 00:54:58,960
let's look at this header right here,

1308
00:54:58,960 --> 00:55:00,400
the one on the envelope.

1309
00:55:00,400 --> 00:55:03,600
It was written by the secretary on Alice's side,

1310
00:55:03,600 --> 00:55:06,480
and it's read by the secretary on Bob's side.

1311
00:55:06,480 --> 00:55:08,720
So it's almost like this header

1312
00:55:08,720 --> 00:55:11,440
is the two secretaries talking to each other,

1313
00:55:11,440 --> 00:55:12,480
in some sense.

1314
00:55:12,480 --> 00:55:13,520
This secretary said,

1315
00:55:13,520 --> 00:55:14,720
hey, give it to Bob.

1316
00:55:14,720 --> 00:55:16,240
And this secretary reads the message,

1317
00:55:16,240 --> 00:55:17,040
oh, it's for Bob.

1318
00:55:17,680 --> 00:55:18,480
And likewise,

1319
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:20,400
if I look at this outermost header,

1320
00:55:20,400 --> 00:55:23,120
this is kind of like the mailman talking to each other.

1321
00:55:23,120 --> 00:55:24,560
So the first mailroom writes,

1322
00:55:24,560 --> 00:55:27,040
oh, it's for Bob's street address.

1323
00:55:27,040 --> 00:55:28,480
And then this one says,

1324
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:30,400
oh, okay, I'm Bob's street address.

1325
00:55:30,400 --> 00:55:32,240
I'll receive this and cast it up.

1326
00:55:32,240 --> 00:55:34,320
So you can almost think of these headers

1327
00:55:34,320 --> 00:55:37,920
as a specific layer talking to its peers

1328
00:55:37,920 --> 00:55:39,440
at the other layer.

1329
00:55:39,440 --> 00:55:40,240
So in some sense,

1330
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:43,120
all the layers are talking to each other

1331
00:55:43,120 --> 00:55:44,480
using the headers.

1332
00:55:44,480 --> 00:55:45,200
That's kind of cool.

1333
00:55:45,760 --> 00:55:47,520
And kind of a consequence of this is

1334
00:55:47,520 --> 00:55:49,760
everyone cares about the header at their layer,

1335
00:55:49,760 --> 00:55:51,520
but they ignore everything else.

1336
00:55:51,520 --> 00:55:53,680
So the mailman cares about street addresses.

1337
00:55:53,680 --> 00:55:57,200
They do not care about what's going on inside the buildings.

1338
00:55:57,200 --> 00:55:57,920
And likewise,

1339
00:55:57,920 --> 00:55:59,920
the secretary cares about the envelope.

1340
00:55:59,920 --> 00:56:03,440
The secretary does not care about the message inside.

1341
00:56:03,440 --> 00:56:04,480
And the CEO,

1342
00:56:04,480 --> 00:56:06,640
they only care about the message inside.

1343
00:56:06,640 --> 00:56:08,160
They don't care about the rest.

1344
00:56:08,160 --> 00:56:12,560
So everyone's working at their own layer.

1345
00:56:12,560 --> 00:56:13,840
That layer has a header.

1346
00:56:13,920 --> 00:56:15,760
And when you put something in that header,

1347
00:56:15,760 --> 00:56:19,600
you're basically talking to your peers at that same layer.

1348
00:56:19,600 --> 00:56:21,040
So that's kind of a nice consequence

1349
00:56:21,040 --> 00:56:22,320
of the way that we built this.

1350
00:56:23,680 --> 00:56:24,000
Okay.

1351
00:56:24,800 --> 00:56:25,840
Something else I want to call out

1352
00:56:25,840 --> 00:56:29,280
when we look at the packets getting passed up and down

1353
00:56:29,280 --> 00:56:31,680
is that there's actually different addressing schemes

1354
00:56:31,680 --> 00:56:33,440
being used at different layers.

1355
00:56:33,440 --> 00:56:35,440
And this is something we'll spend lots of time on.

1356
00:56:35,440 --> 00:56:37,760
But just to give you a quick preview,

1357
00:56:37,760 --> 00:56:39,680
there are different ways to address people.

1358
00:56:39,680 --> 00:56:40,400
So for example,

1359
00:56:41,040 --> 00:56:42,880
the secretaries think about people

1360
00:56:43,520 --> 00:56:44,560
and their names.

1361
00:56:44,560 --> 00:56:45,360
The secretary thinks,

1362
00:56:45,360 --> 00:56:46,160
oh, this is Alice.

1363
00:56:46,160 --> 00:56:47,200
This is Bob.

1364
00:56:47,200 --> 00:56:49,600
Secretary is thinking about people's names.

1365
00:56:49,600 --> 00:56:51,520
By contrast, the mailman,

1366
00:56:51,520 --> 00:56:53,280
mailman is not thinking about names.

1367
00:56:53,280 --> 00:56:55,520
The mailman is thinking in terms of addresses,

1368
00:56:55,520 --> 00:56:57,520
like 123 Alice Street,

1369
00:56:57,520 --> 00:56:59,360
or 456 Bob Street,

1370
00:56:59,360 --> 00:57:01,840
or 2551 Hearst Avenue.

1371
00:57:01,840 --> 00:57:03,360
So just like how in real life,

1372
00:57:03,360 --> 00:57:05,200
we address things differently,

1373
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:06,240
people have names,

1374
00:57:06,240 --> 00:57:08,080
people have street addresses,

1375
00:57:08,080 --> 00:57:10,000
people have office numbers.

1376
00:57:10,000 --> 00:57:11,200
Well, in the internet,

1377
00:57:11,200 --> 00:57:13,360
there are also going to be lots of different ways

1378
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:14,560
to address people

1379
00:57:14,560 --> 00:57:15,680
and different layers

1380
00:57:15,680 --> 00:57:17,840
have different ways of addressing people.

1381
00:57:17,840 --> 00:57:18,800
That's something we'll see.

1382
00:57:20,160 --> 00:57:20,480
Okay.

1383
00:57:21,200 --> 00:57:23,200
So here's the picture one more time.

1384
00:57:23,200 --> 00:57:24,800
But now I'm going to show it in terms of

1385
00:57:24,800 --> 00:57:26,800
the actual layers that we've been seeing.

1386
00:57:26,800 --> 00:57:27,440
So again,

1387
00:57:27,440 --> 00:57:28,800
the application comes up

1388
00:57:28,800 --> 00:57:30,720
with some message that they want to send.

1389
00:57:30,720 --> 00:57:32,000
There's nothing to do with the internet.

1390
00:57:32,000 --> 00:57:33,120
It's just a message.

1391
00:57:33,920 --> 00:57:36,320
And as we move down the stack,

1392
00:57:36,320 --> 00:57:37,680
we add a layer four header.

1393
00:57:37,680 --> 00:57:39,440
Layer four header passes it down.

1394
00:57:39,440 --> 00:57:40,800
Now there's the layer three header

1395
00:57:40,800 --> 00:57:41,760
passes it down.

1396
00:57:41,760 --> 00:57:43,040
Layer two header.

1397
00:57:43,040 --> 00:57:45,040
And now all the headers are wrapped around

1398
00:57:45,600 --> 00:57:48,640
and layer one converts this packet

1399
00:57:48,640 --> 00:57:50,000
to ones and zeros

1400
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:51,600
and sends the ones and zeros

1401
00:57:51,600 --> 00:57:52,800
along a wire

1402
00:57:52,800 --> 00:57:54,240
or an optical fiber

1403
00:57:54,240 --> 00:57:56,000
or a radio wave or whatever.

1404
00:57:56,000 --> 00:57:58,080
So all the headers get wrapped around.

1405
00:57:58,080 --> 00:57:59,760
Layer one converts it to bits,

1406
00:57:59,760 --> 00:58:00,400
transmits it.

1407
00:58:00,400 --> 00:58:03,040
That's like the mail truck driving across town.

1408
00:58:03,760 --> 00:58:07,040
And then this fully encapsulated packet

1409
00:58:07,040 --> 00:58:08,640
with all the headers around it,

1410
00:58:08,640 --> 00:58:12,080
it's received on the other side on a wire.

1411
00:58:12,080 --> 00:58:14,080
And now we can start peeling off the layers

1412
00:58:14,080 --> 00:58:15,120
like an onion.

1413
00:58:15,120 --> 00:58:16,000
So the physical layer,

1414
00:58:16,000 --> 00:58:17,520
it gets the ones and zeros,

1415
00:58:17,520 --> 00:58:19,280
passes it up to the link layer.

1416
00:58:19,280 --> 00:58:22,000
The link layer unwraps its header,

1417
00:58:22,000 --> 00:58:23,840
passes it up to the internet layer.

1418
00:58:23,840 --> 00:58:26,160
The internet layer reads its header

1419
00:58:26,160 --> 00:58:27,280
to understand what to do,

1420
00:58:28,320 --> 00:58:30,880
unwraps it, passes it up to layer four.

1421
00:58:30,880 --> 00:58:32,400
Layer four reads its header,

1422
00:58:32,400 --> 00:58:33,600
learns what to do with it,

1423
00:58:33,600 --> 00:58:35,360
passes it up to layer seven,

1424
00:58:35,360 --> 00:58:37,840
and now layer seven gets the original message again.

1425
00:58:38,400 --> 00:58:40,000
Pretty much what you've already seen,

1426
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:43,040
but showing it in terms of the actual internet layers

1427
00:58:43,040 --> 00:58:44,800
instead of post offices.

1428
00:58:46,320 --> 00:58:46,640
Okay.

1429
00:58:47,360 --> 00:58:48,640
And as mentioned before,

1430
00:58:48,640 --> 00:58:50,240
you can think of the headers

1431
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:51,920
as a specific layer

1432
00:58:51,920 --> 00:58:53,760
talking to its peers

1433
00:58:53,760 --> 00:58:55,120
at that same layer.

1434
00:58:55,120 --> 00:58:56,160
So for example,

1435
00:58:56,160 --> 00:58:58,000
I rewind a little bit

1436
00:58:58,000 --> 00:58:59,120
and I look here.

1437
00:58:59,120 --> 00:59:01,280
When layer three added this header,

1438
00:59:01,280 --> 00:59:03,200
who's going to read this header on the other side?

1439
00:59:03,840 --> 00:59:05,760
The layer three person on the other side

1440
00:59:05,760 --> 00:59:07,760
is going to read this exact header.

1441
00:59:07,760 --> 00:59:09,520
So when you make a layer at header three,

1442
00:59:10,320 --> 00:59:11,520
on the other side,

1443
00:59:11,520 --> 00:59:14,240
it's their layer three implementation

1444
00:59:14,240 --> 00:59:15,680
that's reading that header.

1445
00:59:15,680 --> 00:59:16,480
And likewise,

1446
00:59:16,480 --> 00:59:18,080
when layer four added a header,

1447
00:59:18,080 --> 00:59:20,560
it's for the other side's layer four to read.

1448
00:59:20,560 --> 00:59:21,840
So on some sense,

1449
00:59:21,840 --> 00:59:22,560
on some level,

1450
00:59:22,560 --> 00:59:24,320
you can think of the protocols

1451
00:59:24,320 --> 00:59:26,560
as interacting with their peers

1452
00:59:26,560 --> 00:59:27,920
at the same layer,

1453
00:59:27,920 --> 00:59:29,200
which is kind of useful.

1454
00:59:29,200 --> 00:59:30,560
And these are all fancy acronyms

1455
00:59:30,560 --> 00:59:32,320
for protocols that we're going to see

1456
00:59:32,320 --> 00:59:33,280
throughout the class.

1457
00:59:33,280 --> 00:59:34,560
You don't have to know them now,

1458
00:59:34,560 --> 00:59:36,640
but maybe you recognize a few,

1459
00:59:36,640 --> 00:59:37,520
maybe you don't.

1460
00:59:37,520 --> 00:59:38,800
We'll talk about all of them later,

1461
00:59:38,800 --> 00:59:40,560
so stay tuned.

1462
00:59:42,320 --> 00:59:43,280
Okay, we're almost done.

1463
00:59:44,000 --> 00:59:44,800
So so far,

1464
00:59:44,800 --> 00:59:47,120
I've shown you how the packet travels

1465
00:59:47,120 --> 00:59:49,120
from point A to point B.

1466
00:59:49,120 --> 00:59:50,400
But as we mentioned earlier,

1467
00:59:50,400 --> 00:59:52,640
there's one last piece we have to put together

1468
00:59:52,640 --> 00:59:55,520
so that all the puzzle pieces fit together.

1469
00:59:55,520 --> 00:59:57,760
The last piece we need is that,

1470
00:59:57,760 --> 00:59:58,880
well, it's not the case

1471
00:59:58,880 --> 01:00:01,360
that Alice is directly connected to Bob.

1472
01:00:01,360 --> 01:00:02,240
So remember,

1473
01:00:02,240 --> 01:00:03,520
in the post office,

1474
01:00:03,520 --> 01:00:06,400
things can hop across multiple post offices

1475
01:00:06,400 --> 01:00:08,320
before they reach their destination.

1476
01:00:08,320 --> 01:00:09,280
And in the same way,

1477
01:00:10,880 --> 01:00:13,440
packets can travel across multiple hops,

1478
01:00:13,440 --> 01:00:14,720
multiple routers,

1479
01:00:14,720 --> 01:00:16,640
before they reach their destination.

1480
01:00:16,640 --> 01:00:18,080
So it's not the case that my packet

1481
01:00:18,080 --> 01:00:20,400
just directly goes to its destination,

1482
01:00:20,400 --> 01:00:22,320
like we saw in the example from before,

1483
01:00:22,320 --> 01:00:24,160
like the mail truck just drives straight

1484
01:00:24,160 --> 01:00:25,840
to Bob's building.

1485
01:00:25,840 --> 01:00:26,880
Could be the case

1486
01:00:26,880 --> 01:00:28,560
that we have to take the mail

1487
01:00:28,560 --> 01:00:29,520
to another post office,

1488
01:00:29,520 --> 01:00:30,880
and then they put it on a boat

1489
01:00:30,880 --> 01:00:32,240
and put it on this post,

1490
01:00:32,240 --> 01:00:33,440
send it to this post office,

1491
01:00:33,440 --> 01:00:34,640
and then they put it on a pigeon

1492
01:00:34,640 --> 01:00:36,000
and send it to this post office.

1493
01:00:36,480 --> 01:00:37,440
So it could be the case

1494
01:00:37,440 --> 01:00:40,320
that it has to hop across multiple post offices.

1495
01:00:40,320 --> 01:00:41,360
So we're actually now going to

1496
01:00:42,000 --> 01:00:43,920
do the exact same picture from before,

1497
01:00:44,640 --> 01:00:46,160
Alice sending mail to Bob,

1498
01:00:46,160 --> 01:00:48,240
but now we're going to add post offices

1499
01:00:48,240 --> 01:00:49,200
into the mix

1500
01:00:49,200 --> 01:00:50,320
to get the full picture

1501
01:00:50,320 --> 01:00:52,000
of what sending packets

1502
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:53,760
with lots of headers looks like.

1503
01:00:54,560 --> 01:00:55,520
Okay, so here we go.

1504
01:00:56,080 --> 01:00:57,120
Alice is up here.

1505
01:00:57,120 --> 01:00:58,640
She's the CEO as always.

1506
01:00:58,640 --> 01:01:00,000
She sends a message,

1507
01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:01,520
gives it to the secretary.

1508
01:01:01,520 --> 01:01:03,680
Secretary writes down it's from Alice to Bob.

1509
01:01:04,640 --> 01:01:06,320
Pass it down to the mailman.

1510
01:01:06,320 --> 01:01:08,640
Mailman writes down Alice's street address

1511
01:01:08,640 --> 01:01:09,920
and Bob's street address.

1512
01:01:10,640 --> 01:01:12,560
Pass it down to the post office.

1513
01:01:12,560 --> 01:01:13,760
And here's the new thing

1514
01:01:13,760 --> 01:01:15,520
that we didn't have before.

1515
01:01:15,520 --> 01:01:16,640
This post office says

1516
01:01:16,640 --> 01:01:18,400
on post office number seven,

1517
01:01:18,400 --> 01:01:20,000
I'm not directly connected

1518
01:01:20,000 --> 01:01:21,840
to post office number nine.

1519
01:01:21,840 --> 01:01:24,400
So I can't just send this packet directly to nine.

1520
01:01:24,400 --> 01:01:25,600
I don't have a mailman

1521
01:01:25,600 --> 01:01:28,000
who's going to go to post office nine.

1522
01:01:28,000 --> 01:01:28,960
My carrier pigeons

1523
01:01:28,960 --> 01:01:31,280
are not trained to go to post office nine.

1524
01:01:31,280 --> 01:01:33,040
So instead, I'm going to say,

1525
01:01:33,040 --> 01:01:34,720
well, on post office seven,

1526
01:01:34,720 --> 01:01:36,240
I can't get this directly to nine.

1527
01:01:36,240 --> 01:01:37,600
But you know what I can do?

1528
01:01:37,600 --> 01:01:39,200
I can get it to post office two.

1529
01:01:39,840 --> 01:01:42,480
Post office two is not the final destination,

1530
01:01:42,480 --> 01:01:43,520
but it's closer.

1531
01:01:43,520 --> 01:01:44,960
So I got it closer.

1532
01:01:45,520 --> 01:01:46,880
So we are now adding

1533
01:01:46,880 --> 01:01:48,880
this extra layer of header

1534
01:01:48,880 --> 01:01:50,720
that says this is not going to reach

1535
01:01:50,720 --> 01:01:52,480
its final destination right away,

1536
01:01:52,480 --> 01:01:53,680
but I'll get it closer.

1537
01:01:54,240 --> 01:01:55,360
So post office seven,

1538
01:01:55,920 --> 01:01:57,360
a post office number seven,

1539
01:01:57,360 --> 01:01:59,280
post a letter in an even bigger box

1540
01:01:59,280 --> 01:02:00,960
or like on a truck or something.

1541
01:02:00,960 --> 01:02:02,400
And that bigger box

1542
01:02:02,400 --> 01:02:04,320
is destined for post office number two.

1543
01:02:04,880 --> 01:02:06,480
So it's still wrapped in a bunch of layers.

1544
01:02:06,480 --> 01:02:08,880
We just wrapped it around one extra layer

1545
01:02:08,880 --> 01:02:10,160
that we weren't showing earlier.

1546
01:02:10,960 --> 01:02:12,640
Now this goes to post office two.

1547
01:02:12,640 --> 01:02:14,240
But what does post office two do?

1548
01:02:14,240 --> 01:02:16,320
Well, remember this outer layer

1549
01:02:16,320 --> 01:02:18,800
is for the post offices to talk to each other.

1550
01:02:18,800 --> 01:02:20,800
So we unwrap that layer,

1551
01:02:20,800 --> 01:02:23,120
exposing the headers inside,

1552
01:02:23,120 --> 01:02:24,800
and the post office looks at this and says,

1553
01:02:25,440 --> 01:02:27,760
I'm not four, five, six Bob Street.

1554
01:02:27,760 --> 01:02:28,480
It's not for me.

1555
01:02:29,360 --> 01:02:31,920
But this needs to reach four, five, six Bob Street.

1556
01:02:31,920 --> 01:02:33,200
How does it get there?

1557
01:02:33,200 --> 01:02:34,800
